{"name":"Green Jalapeño","hex":"#8c8e51","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"},{"term":"jalapeño","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cultivar of hot chili pepper, Capsicum annuum."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jalape%C3%B1o"}],"entries":[{"title":"Jalapeño","blocks":[{"text":"The jalapeño is a medium-sized chili pepper pod type cultivar of the species Capsicum annuum. A mature jalapeño chili is 5–10 cm (2–4 in) long and 25–38 mm wide, and hangs down from the plant. The pungency of jalapeño peppers varies, but is usually between 4,000 and 8,500 units on the Scoville scale. Commonly picked and consumed while still green, it is occasionally allowed to fully ripen and turn red, orange, or yellow. It is wider and generally milder than the similar Serrano pepper."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Immature_jalapeno_capsicum_annuum_var_annuum.jpeg/330px-Immature_jalapeno_capsicum_annuum_var_annuum.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jalape%C3%B1o","wikidata":"Q504133"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/JalapenoPeppers.JPG/330px-JalapenoPeppers.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/JalapenoPeppers.JPG/330px-JalapenoPeppers.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Five (5) Jalapeño peppers","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJalapenoPeppers.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Bowl_of_jalape%C3%B1os.jpg/500px-Bowl_of_jalape%C3%B1os.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Bowl_of_jalape%C3%B1os.jpg/1280px-Bowl_of_jalape%C3%B1os.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Bowl_of_jalape%C3%B1os.jpg/500px-Bowl_of_jalape%C3%B1os.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Jalapeños grown in an Oklahoma garden. Red jalapeños are used to make sriracha sauce.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABowl_of_jalape%C3%B1os.jpg"},{"srcset":"https: